HYBRID LICENSE AND DISTRIBUTION NOTICE
======================================

This repository contains software components with different copyright statuses
and license terms.

The ELAS3D-XTAL code combines original research software, modifications, and
extensions developed by the authors of this repository with software derived
from finite-element elasticity code originally developed by the National
Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST), as described in NISTIR 6269.

The original NIST material is a work of the U.S. Federal Government and is not
subject to copyright protection in the United States under 17 U.S.C. § 105.
The new contributions and modifications developed by the authors of this
repository are distributed under the MIT License.

Please refer to the sections below for the terms governing each component.

========================================================================
PART 1: ELAS3D-XTAL NEW CONTRIBUTIONS AND MODIFICATIONS
========================================================================

The following terms apply to all original files, modifications, extensions,
documentation, scripts, and algorithms developed by the authors of this
repository, including but not limited to:

1. ELAS3D-XTAL Fortran solver modifications and extensions.
2. OpenMP acceleration and parallelization routines.
3. Anisotropic polycrystalline elasticity implementation.
4. Grain-wise crystallographic orientation handling using Rodrigues vectors.
5. Cubic single-crystal stiffness rotation and Bond-matrix transformation
   routines.
6. HDF5 input/output implementations for phase labels, grain orientations,
   full-field stress tensors, and von Mises stress fields.
7. Preconditioned Conjugate Gradient, PCG, solver enhancements, including
   block-Jacobi preconditioning and residual-safeguard logic.
8. Active-node masking for pore/void-only regions.
9. Full-field stress and von Mises stress post-processing routines.
10. MATLAB-based microstructure generation, pore processing, mesh/export, and
    fatigue-life prediction tools included in this repository.
11. Documentation, examples, comments, input/output templates, and auxiliary
    scripts created by the repository authors.

